        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anomalous development of spinal structures, which are apparent at birth or within the first weeks of life.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Malformation of the occipital bones, atlas, and axis; malformation of the odontoid process; occipitoatlantoaxial malformation; and occipital dysplasia—may cause atlantoaxial subluxation with secondary compression and trauma to the first segments of the cervical spinal cord.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Other embryonic or developmental anomalies of the vertebrae such as hemivertebra, transitional vertebra, block vertebra, and butterfly vertebra—these defects cause deformity and instability of the vertebral canal and, on rare occasions, compression of the associated spinal cord or nerve roots.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sacrococcygeal dysgenesis—characterized by absence or partial development of the sacrocaudal spinal cord segments; often associated with additional malformations (e.g., spina bifida).",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Spina bifida—caused by failure of fusion of the vertebral arches; may be associated with protrusion of the spinal cord and meninges; other malformations often linked to this syndrome include spinal dysplasia, dysraphism, syringomyelia/hydromyelia, and myelodysplasia.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital spinal stenosis—can occur when vertebral malformations cause segmental or diffuse narrowing of the spinal cord; inborn errors in skeletal growth, hypertrophy of ligamentum flavum, and bony proliferation may also contribute to the stenosis.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nervous—spinal cord; spinal nerve roots; and vertebral column.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GENETICS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "GENETICS",
          "canonical_heading": "GENETICS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A genetic background, with unknown mode of inheritance, is suspected in most congenital spinal diseases.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sacrococcygeal dysgenesis—autosomal dominant.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thoracic hemivertebra of German shorthaired pointers—autosomal recessive.",
              "pdf_page": 395,
              "printed_page": 313,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
